.TH SFTPCTL 8 "2026-09-08" "sftpctl 1.0" "System Administration Commands"
.SH NAME
sftpctl \- manage chrooted, shell-less SFTP/SCP accounts with disk quotas
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sftpctl
.I command
.RI [ arguments ]
.RI [ options ]
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sftpctl
creates and manages user accounts that can transfer files over
.BR scp (1)
or
.BR sftp (1)
and do nothing else: no shell, no port forwarding, and no visibility outside
their own directory. Each account is confined by
.B ChrootDirectory
and capped by a per-user disk quota.
.PP
Accounts authenticate by password, public key, or both. The relevant
.B sshd
directives are written inside a
.B "Match Group"
block, so they apply only to these accounts and leave a hardened global
configuration intact for ordinary logins.
.
.SH QUICK START
Determine whether the account directory has its own filesystem. Quotas are a
per-filesystem mechanism, so this decides everything that follows.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
sudo findmnt \-no SOURCE,TARGET \-\-target /home
.EE
.RE
.PP
If
.B TARGET
is
.BR /home ,
enable quotas on it and proceed. If it is
.BR / ,
create a dedicated filesystem first with
.BR "sftpctl provision" .
See
.B THE ROOT FILESYSTEM PROBLEM
below.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
sudo sftpctl provision /srv/sftp 10G
sudo sftpctl setup /srv/sftp \-\-auth=both
sudo sftpctl add alice 5 \-\-pass \-\-base=/srv/sftp
.EE
.RE
.
.SH COMMANDS
.
.SS volumes
.B sftpctl volumes
.PP
Lists every volume group with size, free space, and logical-volume count, shows
which one auto-selection would pick, and lists existing sftpctl volumes. On a
server with several volume groups, run this before
.BR provision .
Aliased as
.BR vgs .
.
.SS config
.B sftpctl config
.RB [ show | init ]
.PP
.B show
(the default) prints every tunable with its effective value and where it came
from.
.B init
writes a commented
.I /etc/sftpctl.conf
with the current values, pre-filling
.B DEFAULT_VG
with the volume group that has the most free space.
.PP
Precedence is environment, then config file, then built-in default. Note that
.BR sudo (8)
strips the environment by default, so an exported variable will not reach
.B sftpctl
unless you use
.B sudo -E
or set it inline.
.
.SS provision
.B sftpctl provision
.I mountpoint size
.RB [ \-\-lv | \-\-loop | \-\-vg=\c
.IR NAME ]
.PP
Creates a dedicated filesystem for SFTP accounts, formatted with the ext4 quota
feature already enabled, adds an
.BR fstab (5)
entry after backing up the original, and mounts it.
.TP
.B \-\-lv
New LVM logical volume from free volume-group space.
.TP
.BI \-\-vg= NAME
Use a specific volume group. Implies
.BR \-\-lv .
.TP
.B \-\-loop
Sparse image file at
.IR /var/lib/sftpctl/sftpdata.img .
.PP
With no mode given, an LV is created in the volume group with the most free
space, falling back to a loop image if LVM is unavailable. A
.B DEFAULT_VG
in the config file overrides auto-selection;
.B \-\-vg
overrides both.
.PP
Free space is compared before
.BR lvcreate (8)
runs, so an oversized request aborts with the available groups listed rather
than failing partway through.
.PP
Prefer
.B \-\-lv
when space allows: a real block device, no stacked filesystems, and growable
later with
.BR lvextend (8)
and
.BR resize2fs (8).
The loop image is sparse, so the host filesystem can fill underneath it and
accounts will hit
.B ENOSPC
while still under quota.
.
.SS setup
.B sftpctl setup
.RI [ mountpoint ]
.RB [ \-\-auth=key | password | both ]
.PP
Run once per server. Verifies quota is active on the target filesystem, creates
the
.B sftpusers
group, and writes
.IR /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/60-sftp-only.conf .
.TP
.B \-\-auth=key
Public keys only.
.TP
.B \-\-auth=password
Passwords only.
.TP
.B \-\-auth=both
Either works. This is the default.
.PP
Re-running backs up the existing drop-in first. If
.B "sshd -t"
fails, the change is reverted and nothing is applied.
.
.SS add
.B sftpctl add
.I user
.RI [ size ]
.RI [ options ]
.PP
Creates an account, its chroot tree, and its quota.
.TP
.B \-\-pass
Set a password, generated and printed once.
.TP
.B \-\-ask
Prompt for the password, twice, without echoing.
.TP
.BI \-\-pass= SECRET
Set a specific password. This lands in shell history and the process table;
prefer
.BR \-\-ask .
.TP
.BI \-\-key= STRING | FILE
Authorize a public key at creation.
.TP
.BI \-\-base= DIR
Parent directory. Defaults to
.B DEFAULT_BASE
.RI ( /home
unless configured).
.TP
.BI \-\-expire= DAYS
Set an account expiry date.
.PP
Sizes accept
.BR 10 ,
.BR 500M ,
.BR 10G ,
.BR 2T .
A bare number means gigabytes.
.PP
Creating an account with neither
.B \-\-pass
nor
.B \-\-key
is permitted but warns: it has no way to log in until credentials are added.
.
.SS quota
.B sftpctl quota
.I user size
.PP
Changes a quota. Takes effect on the next write \(em no logout, no service
reload, no account changes.
.PP
Shrinking below current usage is permitted and warns rather than blocking.
Existing files remain; the user cannot write again until they delete something.
.
.SS passwd
.B sftpctl passwd
.I user
.RB [ \-\-ask | \-\-stdin | \-\-gen | \-\-lock | \-\-unlock | \fIpassword\fR ]
.PP
With no argument or
.BR \-\-gen ,
generates and prints a new password.
.B \-\-ask
prompts twice without echoing.
.B \-\-stdin
reads one line from standard input, for scripted use.
.B \-\-lock
disables password authentication while leaving key authentication intact;
.B \-\-unlock
reverses it.
.PP
Password aging is disabled on every password this command sets. These accounts
have no shell, so a user can never run
.BR passwd (1);
an expiring password would lock them out with no recovery path.
.
.SS addkey, delkey
.B sftpctl addkey
.I user
.RB { \fIpubkey\fR | \fIfile\fR | \- }
.br
.B sftpctl delkey
.I user
.RI [ match ]
.PP
.B addkey
accepts a key as a literal string, a file path, or
.B \-
for standard input. Duplicates are detected and skipped.
.B delkey
with no match presents a numbered picker; with a match it removes every key
containing that string.
.
.SS list, show
.B sftpctl list
.br
.B sftpctl show
.I user
.PP
.B list
tabulates usage, quota, percentage, and authentication method for every account,
flagging any past 90 percent or lacking credentials.
.B show
gives the same detail for one account plus its home, shell, and expiry.
.
.SS disable, enable
.B sftpctl disable
.I user
.br
.B sftpctl enable
.I user
.PP
.B disable
locks the password and moves
.I authorized_keys
aside. Data and quota are untouched, so a disabled account still occupies disk.
Use
.B remove
to reclaim space.
.
.SS remove
.B sftpctl remove
.I user
.RB [ \-\-purge ]
.PP
Deletes the account. Without
.BR \-\-purge ,
the home directory and its contents are kept. Requires typing the username to
confirm.
.PP
The quota is zeroed before
.BR userdel (8).
Otherwise the limit stays bound to the freed UID, and whichever account next
reuses that UID silently inherits it.
.
.SS check
.B sftpctl check
.PP
Audits sshd configuration, chroot ownership and permissions, shell assignment,
credentials, and quota state across all accounts. Run after any manual change to
.I /etc/ssh
or the account directories.
.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/sftpctl.conf
Configuration. Shell syntax, sourced at startup. Create with
.BR "sftpctl config init" .
.TP
.I /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/60-sftp-only.conf
The
.B "Match Group"
block. Written by
.BR setup ,
backed up before any rewrite.
.TP
.I /var/lib/sftpctl/sftpdata.img
Backing image when provisioned with
.BR \-\-loop .
.TP
.I /etc/fstab
Amended by
.BR provision ,
with a timestamped backup alongside.
.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
All of these may also be set in
.IR /etc/sftpctl.conf .
The environment takes precedence.
.TP
.B DEFAULT_VG
Volume group used by
.BR provision .
Unset means auto-select by free space.
.TP
.B DEFAULT_BASE
Parent directory for new accounts. Default
.IR /home .
.TP
.B DEFAULT_QUOTA_GB
Quota when
.B add
is given no size. Default 10.
.TP
.B SFTP_GROUP
Group the Match block targets. Default
.BR sftpusers .
Changing this after accounts exist orphans them.
.TP
.B UPLOAD_SUBDIR
Writable directory inside each chroot. Default
.BR upload .
.TP
.B SOFT_PCT
Soft limit as a percentage of hard. Default 90.
.TP
.B INODES_PER_GB
Inode cap scaling. Default 20000.
.TP
.B PASS_LENGTH
Generated password length. Default 20.
.TP
.B SFTPCTL_CONFIG
Alternate config file path.
.
.SH DIRECTORY LAYOUT
.EX
/srv/sftp/alice          root:root   755   chroot root, NOT user-writable
|-- .ssh/
|   \\-- authorized_keys  alice:alice 600   read by sshd as root, pre-chroot
\\-- upload/              alice:alice 700   where files land
.EE
.PP
.B ChrootDirectory
requires the directory and every parent to be owned by root with no group or
world write. The user therefore cannot write to their own home directory, which
is why
.I upload/
exists inside it. Senders target
.IR upload/ ,
relative to the chroot; there is no absolute server path they can reach.
.PP
This is the most common misconfiguration, and sshd's response when it is wrong
.RB ( "Connection closed" )
identifies nothing.
.B "sftpctl check"
catches it.
.
.SH THE ROOT FILESYSTEM PROBLEM
On a stock Ubuntu LVM install
.I /home
is a directory on
.IR / ,
not a mount point. Quotas cannot be used there, for two independent reasons.
.PP
First, enabling ext4 quotas means
.BR "tune2fs -O quota" ,
which requires the filesystem to be unmounted. A running root filesystem cannot
be unmounted; the only offline path is rescue media. The deprecated alternative,
external
.I aquota.user
and
.I aquota.group
files enabled by a
.B usrquota
mount option, is refused by current kernels with
.BR "Device or resource busy" .
.PP
Second, and more fundamentally, a user quota on
.I /
limits that UID everywhere on
.IR / ,
including
.I /tmp
and
.IR /var/tmp ,
not merely the upload directory. Even if it could be enabled, it is the wrong
mechanism.
.PP
.B "sftpctl provision"
resolves both by giving the accounts their own filesystem, formatted with the
quota feature from the outset.
.
.SH VERIFYING QUOTAS
Use
.BR repquota (8),
not
.BR quotaon (8):
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
sudo repquota \-u /srv/sftp
.EE
.RE
.PP
A table with grace times and a
.B root
row means quotas are working, even when otherwise empty. That is the
authoritative test.
.PP
.B "quotaon -p"
reports unreliably on a filesystem using the ext4 quota feature, because the
kernel manages quotas through hidden inodes rather than the interface
.B quotaon
was built around. For the same reason
.I /proc/mounts
will not show a
.B usrquota
option; its absence is expected.
.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Fresh server, /home on the root filesystem
.EX
sudo findmnt \-no SOURCE,TARGET \-\-target /home     # returns /
sudo sftpctl volumes                              # inspect volume groups
sudo sftpctl config init                          # pin DEFAULT_VG, DEFAULT_BASE
sudo sftpctl provision /srv/sftp 100G
sudo sftpctl setup /srv/sftp \-\-auth=both
sudo sftpctl add alice 10 \-\-pass
.EE
.
.SS Fresh server, /home already a separate filesystem
.EX
sudo systemctl stop sshd
sudo umount /home
sudo tune2fs \-O quota \-Q usrquota,grpquota /dev/mapper/vg\-home
sudo mount /home
sudo systemctl start sshd
sudo sftpctl setup \-\-auth=both
sudo sftpctl add alice 10 \-\-pass
.EE
.
.SS Large server with several volume groups
.EX
sudo sftpctl volumes
sudo sftpctl provision /srv/sftp 500G \-\-vg=data\-vg
sudo sftpctl config init          # records DEFAULT_VG for later
sudo sftpctl config show
.EE
.
.SS Key-only vendor account, expiring after 90 days
.EX
sudo sftpctl setup /srv/sftp \-\-auth=key
sudo sftpctl add vendor 50 \-\-key=/root/vendor.pub \-\-expire=90
.EE
.
.SS Password account for a partner who cannot manage keys
.EX
sudo sftpctl setup /srv/sftp \-\-auth=both
sudo sftpctl add partner 20 \-\-pass
# password is printed once; record it before clearing the screen
.EE
.PP
To choose the password yourself without it reaching shell history:
.EX
sudo sftpctl add partner 20 \-\-ask
sudo sftpctl passwd partner \-\-ask
.EE
.PP
From a script or secrets store:
.EX
pass\-cli show sftp/partner | sudo sftpctl passwd partner \-\-stdin
.EE
.
.SS Adding a key to an existing password account
.EX
sudo sftpctl addkey partner /root/partner.pub
sudo sftpctl passwd partner \-\-lock        # keys only from now on
sudo sftpctl show partner
.EE
.
.SS Rotating a forgotten password
.EX
sudo sftpctl passwd alice \-\-gen
.EE
.
.SS "event not found" when typing a password
An unquoted
.B !
triggers
.BR bash (1)
history expansion before
.B sftpctl
ever runs. Single-quote it, or avoid the command line entirely:
.EX
sudo sftpctl passwd alice 'Aa@!23456'
sudo sftpctl passwd alice \-\-ask
.EE
.
.SS Changing a quota
.EX
sudo sftpctl show alice                   # check usage before shrinking
sudo sftpctl quota alice 500M
sudo repquota \-u /srv/sftp                # verify independently
.EE
.
.SS Finding who is filling the disk
.EX
sudo sftpctl list                         # accounts past 90% are flagged
sudo du \-sh /srv/sftp/*/upload | sort \-h
.EE
.
.SS Suspending and restoring an account
.EX
sudo sftpctl disable alice
sudo sftpctl enable alice
.EE
.
.SS Offboarding
.EX
sudo tar czf /backup/alice.tgz \-C /srv/sftp/alice upload
sudo sftpctl remove alice \-\-purge
.EE
.
.SS Growing the volume when it fills
.EX
sudo vgs                                  # confirm free extents
sudo lvextend \-L +50G /dev/data\-vg/sftpdata
sudo resize2fs /dev/data\-vg/sftpdata
df \-h /srv/sftp
.EE
.
.SS Auditing after a manual change
.EX
sudo sftpctl check
.EE
.
.SH TROUBLESHOOTING
.
.SS Connection closes immediately after authentication
The chroot directory is not owned by
.B root:root
or is group- or world-writable. This is by far the most common cause.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
sudo sftpctl check
sudo chown root:root /srv/sftp/alice && sudo chmod 755 /srv/sftp/alice
.EE
.RE
.
.SS Password is rejected but a key works
Either a global
.B "PasswordAuthentication no"
is taking effect, or
.B "UsePAM no"
is blocking PAM-mediated password authentication.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
sudo sftpctl setup /srv/sftp \-\-auth=both
sudo sshd \-T | grep \-i \-e passwordauth \-e usepam
.EE
.RE
.
.SS scp fails but sftp works
The client is forcing the legacy SCP protocol with
.BR "scp -O" ,
which requires a real shell. OpenSSH 9.0 and later use the SFTP protocol by
default and work correctly. Remove the flag, or upgrade the client.
.
.SS "subsystem request failed"
The
.B Subsystem
line is missing, or points at an external
.B sftp-server
binary that does not exist inside the chroot. Only
.B internal-sftp
works with
.BR ChrootDirectory .
.
.SS Permission denied when writing
The sender is targeting the chroot root instead of
.IR upload/ .
The chroot root is deliberately not writable.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
scp file.tar alice@server:upload/          # correct
scp file.tar alice@server:/               # fails
.EE
.RE
.
.SS Transfer stops partway with no clear error
The quota was reached. SFTP reports this as a generic failure, and the partial
file remains on disk still consuming quota.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
sudo sftpctl show alice
.EE
.RE
.
.SS Account worked yesterday and fails today
The password expired.
.B sftpctl
disables aging on passwords it sets, but a password set by another tool, or a
.B PASS_MAX_DAYS
policy applied afterwards, will expire. Since the account has no shell, the user
cannot change it themselves.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
sudo chage \-l alice
sudo sftpctl passwd alice \-\-gen
.EE
.RE
.
.SS "quotaon: Device or resource busy"
The kernel is refusing deprecated external
.I aquota.*
files. Clean up and use
.B provision
instead.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
sudo quotaoff \-a 2>/dev/null; sudo rm \-f /aquota.user /aquota.group
.EE
.RE
.
.SS "tune2fs failed" during setup
The target filesystem is mounted. The ext4 quota feature bit can only be set
offline. See
.BR "THE ROOT FILESYSTEM PROBLEM" .
.
.SS list shows 0K / 0K for every account
A pre-1.0 version parsing
.BR quota (1)
rather than
.BR repquota (8).
.BR quota (1)
prints the device in its first column, not the mount point, so the match
silently failed and every figure read as zero. Upgrade.
.
.SS "insufficient free space" naming an unexpected volume group
More than one volume group exists and auto-selection chose differently than you
expected.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
sudo sftpctl volumes
sudo sftpctl provision /srv/sftp 100G \-\-vg=data\-vg
.EE
.RE
.
.SS Watching a live login attempt
.EX
sudo journalctl \-u sshd \-f
sudo /usr/sbin/sshd \-d \-p 2222        # debug mode, alternate port
.EE
.
.SH SECURITY NOTES
Quotas cap consumption; they do not reserve it. A 10 GB quota on a 10 GB volume
lets a single account fill the entire filesystem. With several accounts, size
each at a fraction of the volume so one sender cannot starve the others.
.PP
Each account receives an inode cap alongside its space cap, 20000 per gigabyte
by default. Without one, an account can exhaust the filesystem's inodes with
millions of empty files while remaining well under its space quota.
.PP
Generated passwords are shown once and stored only as a hash. They cannot be
recovered, only replaced.
.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Error. The condition is described on standard error; where a failure occurs
partway, the command reports what was and was not applied.
.
